  "name": "Salivary glands",
  "subtitle": "Saliva, amylase and the first enzyme of digestion",
  "processes": [
   "digestion"
  ],
  "learn": {
   "exam": [
    "The salivary glands secrete saliva into the mouth; they are associated organs, so they are not part of the alimentary canal itself.",
    "Saliva contains the enzyme salivary amylase, which begins the chemical digestion of starch.",
    "Amylase breaks starch down into maltose, a simple reducing sugar. (Core answer: ‘simple reducing sugars’; Supplement answer: ‘maltose’ — give the name if you sit Paper 4.)",
    "Salivary amylase works best at about pH 6.8, which is close to neutral and is the pH of saliva.",
    "Saliva also lubricates the food, so the bolus is slippery and can be swallowed easily.",
    {
     "text": "Maltose is not absorbed in the mouth; it is broken down to glucose later by maltase on the epithelium of the small intestine.",
     "sup": true
    }
   ],
   "golden": "Salivary amylase produces maltose, not glucose; glucose only appears when maltase acts on maltose at the epithelium of the small intestine.",
   "further": [
    {
     "ref": "5.1 Enzymes",
     "text": "Salivary amylase is your first meeting with an enzyme in action. Topic 5 explains in full why it only works near pH 7 and why heat or acid denatures it."
    },
    {
     "ref": "Beyond 0610",
     "text": "Saliva is over 99% water; the rest is mucus, mineral ions, amylase and lysozyme, an enzyme that damages bacterial cell walls."
    },
    {
     "ref": "Beyond 0610",
     "text": "Food is in the mouth for well under a minute, so most salivary amylase actually works inside the bolus after swallowing, until stomach acid penetrates the bolus and denatures it."
    },
    {
     "ref": "Beyond 0610",
     "text": "Saliva contains hydrogencarbonate ions that buffer the acid made by plaque bacteria, which is part of the reason a dry mouth leads to more tooth decay."
    }
   ]
  },
